## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Shutdown preserves IDs, frozen contracts, grants, liabilities, monitor intent and notice state. Restart discovers them without implicitly acquiring ownership, submitting or restarting monitoring.
- [ ] An explicit conversational resume lets the assistant reacquire eligible ownership and observe the existing saved run without a new create/reservation or manual copied command.
- [ ] Competing runtimes cannot own duplicate active monitors. Recover only proven-dead owners; age alone does not authorize takeover. Session/project targeting is rechecked at actual delivery.
- [ ] Exercise crash points around create acknowledgement, remote-ID publication, terminal receipt, notice scheduling and delivery acknowledgement. Outcomes remain inspectable and durable pending notices are recoverable.
- [ ] Where host delivery acknowledgement is ambiguous, retain pending/uncertain delivery for reconciliation instead of claiming exactly-once across crashes or silently dropping results.
- [ ] Switch/fork/tree/archive and late callbacks cannot reactivate a detached context, overwrite another plan or route results into another conversation. Explicit archive stops monitoring/delivery but preserves history and warns about outstanding spending.
- [ ] Late original completion closes further submission eligibility; specifically approved replacement histories retain both genuine successes and separate costs without another automatic run.
- [ ] Verify missing-ID uncertainty, paused monitors and local-invalid completed results remain recoverable within their original contracts. Resume never infers spending consent.
- [ ] Add deterministic real-filesystem/public lifecycle failure and concurrency regressions; keep each failure distinguishable from actual remote failure.

